// platform domain -- Go-backed capabilities over the platform's own rows.

// ---- deploy from the Library (memql#4345) ----
//
// Deploying a hosted site is a read-then-validate-then-write dance MemQL
// cannot express on its own: it resolves a Library artifact to its backing
// file row, fetches that file's bytes from object storage, expands a zip,
// enforces size / count limits over the expanded tree, uploads every file
// under a fresh content-addressed version prefix, and only then flips the
// site row's bundleRef. The Go side is component/sitepublish/site_publish.go;
// the atomic upload-then-flip half is component/edge's Publisher, the same
// one POST /sites/{id}/bundles uses, so a portal deploy and a CI deploy
// produce the same versioned prefixes and can roll back across each other.
//
// AUTHORIZATION IS NOT IN THIS DECLARATION and is not meant to be. The
// capability runs under the CALLER's actor and issues owner-scoped named
// queries (siteById, libraryArtifactById, libraryFileById) plus one guarded
// write (updateSiteBundle against v1:platform:site's composite tier), so the
// caller must own BOTH the site -- or be a cluster owner -- and the artifact.
// A cross-user call resolves zero rows and is refused by name.
@sdk
@executor("integration.sitePublish.fromArtifact")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Deploy a Library zip artifact to one of the caller's hosted sites (memql#4345). The caller must own the site (or be a cluster owner) AND own the artifact, which must be a Library file whose MIME type is a zip. The bundle is read from object storage and validated -- index.html at the ROOT for spa and shopify_storefront, plus the same per-file (25 MB), whole-bundle (500 MB) and file-count (20000) limits POST /sites/{id}/bundles enforces -- then written under a new content-addressed version prefix before bundleRef is flipped, so a failed publish leaves the site serving exactly what it was serving. artifactId is stamped on the site row as provenance and the attempt is recorded on the security audit log. Returns {siteId, artifactId, fileId, version, bundleRef, fileCount, totalBytes}. Rollback is unchanged: updateSiteBundle pointed back at an earlier version's bundleRef.")
builtin sitePublishFromArtifact {
  siteId      string!  @description("The v1:platform:site row to publish to.")
  artifactId  string!  @description("The v1:library:artifact index row of the zip to deploy.")
}

// ---- Custom domains (epic memql#4805) ----
//
// Two capabilities and a sweep. The split between them is the same one
// sitePublishFromArtifact already draws: a write MemQL cannot express reaches
// the graph through a builtin, and everything the graph CAN express stays a
// mutation.
//
// AUTHORIZATION IS NOT IN THESE DECLARATIONS. v1:platform:customDomain declares
// @rowAuthz(clusterOwner), so every read and write below runs against that tier
// under the CALLER's actor -- an admin who is not a cluster owner resolves zero
// rows and is refused by name. Design D1 is deliberate about the scope: v1
// automates what was already owner territory rather than widening it, because
// certificate issuance and Ingress objects are cluster-level resources with
// real rate limits.

/// The reachable half of creating a binding: mint the ownership token, prefill the account tie, and
/// write the row. `createCustomDomain` itself is @serverOnly precisely so this is the only way in --
/// a caller who chooses their own verification token proves nothing by publishing it.
@sdk
@executor("integration.customDomain.add")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Bind a client's own domain to one of this cluster's deployables (epic memql#4805). Mints the ownership token the client publishes at TXT _memql-verify.<hostname>, prefills the account tie from the site when one exists, and lands the row `pending_dns`. Three guards refuse before anything is written: a hostname under the cluster's own domain (that is <slug>.<domain> territory and createSite owns it), a collision with a front-door host or an existing site or binding, and a claim past MEMQL_CUSTOM_DOMAIN_MAX_PER_SITE. Nothing is issued until BOTH DNS checks pass -- Let's Encrypt rate limits are real, and a domain that merely points at this cluster must not be claimable by whoever asks first. Returns {domainId, hostname, siteId, token, status, verifyRecordName, pointsToTarget, pointsToKind}.")
builtin customDomainAdd {
  siteId    string!  @description("The v1:platform:site row this domain should serve.")
  hostname  string!  @description("The client's own fully qualified host, e.g. www.acme.com or acme.com.")
}

/// One reconciliation pass over every non-terminal binding. The scheduled automation's whole body.
///
/// It does the DNS half itself and dispatches the Kubernetes half through the capability-script
/// seam, which is what keeps client-go out of the engine's module graph (design D2). Both are one
/// builtin because they are one decision: the check's result IS what selects the next action, and
/// splitting them would put the state machine in two places that could disagree about a row.
@executor("integration.customDomain.reconcile")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Run one custom-domain reconciliation pass (epic memql#4805). For each non-terminal v1:platform:customDomain row: verify the TXT ownership token and the pointing record, promote to `issuing` when both pass, dispatch the idempotent bind capability script for `issuing` rows and the unbind script for `removing` ones, and write back what was seen with a typed failureReason. Retries ride this schedule; there is no manual re-check. Returns {checked, verified, issued, removed, failed}.")
builtin customDomainReconcile { }
// ---- the D10 lifecycle capabilities (epic memql#4794) ----
//
// Archiving is the one lifecycle edge that is not a plain status write, and
// these two exist for exactly the parts a mutation body cannot express:
// comparing a typed confirmation against the STORED name, and asking a question
// about OTHER rows ("does this package still have live deployables?").
//
// AUTHORIZATION IS NOT IN THESE DECLARATIONS and is not meant to be, exactly as
// sitePublishFromArtifact records above. Each runs under the CALLER's actor and
// resolves its target through an owner-scoped named query first, so a caller
// who does not own the row -- and is not a cluster owner -- resolves zero rows
// and is refused by name before anything is written. The status write itself is
// then guarded a second time beside executeWrite, which is what makes the
// structural law (disable-first, systemOwned exemption, no resurrection from
// archived to live) true for every writer rather than only for these.

@sdk
@executor("integration.packages.archiveSite")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Archive one of the caller's sites (epic memql#4794, D10). Refuses unless the site is already DISABLED -- archiving is the end of a lifecycle, not a shortcut past pausing -- and unless confirmHostname matches the site's stored hostname exactly, which is the typed confirmation the OS renders and this server verifies. A systemOwned site (the cluster's own portal and OS) is refused outright: those rows are exempt from the lifecycle entirely. An archived site stops serving (404) but stays listed behind the Archived filter, because an archive is a place and not a void. Returns {siteId, hostname, status}. Refusals carry stable codes.")
builtin siteArchive {
  siteId           string!  @description("The v1:platform:site row to archive.")
  confirmHostname  string!  @description("The site's own hostname, typed by the person as confirmation. Compared against the stored value; a mismatch refuses and writes nothing.")
}

@sdk
@executor("integration.packages.restoreSite")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Bring one of the caller's archived sites back to DISABLED (epic memql#4794, D10). Deliberately not back to live: restoring returns a site to the state it was archived from, and publishing it again is a separate, visible decision. No typed confirmation, because leaving the archive destroys nothing. Returns {siteId, hostname, status}.")
builtin siteRestore {
  siteId  string!  @description("The archived v1:platform:site row to restore.")
}

@sdk
@executor("integration.packages.archivePackage")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Archive one of the caller's packages AND every app it produced (epic memql#4794 D10; the cascade is epic memql#4885, 'archive this source and every app it produced'). Refuses with package_has_active_deployables, naming only the LIVE hostnames and before any site is touched, while any app this package deployed is still serving -- a package is the source its deployables came from, and filing it away while one is still serving the internet would put the record and the reality in different states; pausing first is the step that gives anyone still using it a chance to notice, and it stays the person's decision. Every paused app is archived, and a never-published (draft) app is walked through disabled first, each through the same guarded status write siteArchive uses, sites first and the package last; a write the guard refuses surfaces and stops the cascade there. Apps already archived are left alone. Also refuses unless confirmName matches the package's stored name exactly. packageRestore does NOT cascade back. Archived packages stay listed behind the Archived filter. Returns {packageId, name, status, archivedSites}.")
builtin packageArchive {
  packageId    string!  @description("The v1:platform:package row to archive.")
  confirmName  string!  @description("The package's own name, typed by the person as confirmation. Compared against the stored value; a mismatch refuses and writes nothing.")
}

@sdk
@executor("integration.packages.restorePackage")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Bring one of the caller's archived packages back to active (epic memql#4794, D10). No typed confirmation and no cascade: the package's sites keep whatever status they have, because restoring a source is not a decision to redeploy it. Returns {packageId, name, status}.")
builtin packageRestore {
  packageId  string!  @description("The archived v1:platform:package row to restore.")
}

// ---- traffic and health (epic memql#4906, the Run epic) ----
//
// The read half of the edge's request log. It lives in PLATFORM rather than
// in observability, with the row shape it answers in, because what it is
// gated on is the SITE: a caller reads a deployable's traffic exactly when
// they may read the deployable. The rows are observability's; the
// authorization is platform's, and the authorization is what decides where a
// capability belongs.
//
// A BUILTIN RATHER THAN A QUERY, and codeMetricsInWindow is the cautionary
// case: a DSL `query` reads the graph, these rows live in a dedicated
// TimescaleDB relation with no concept mapping, and a query over one answers
// nothing at all. The working shape for a read served from Go is the @sdk
// builtin whose executor returns synthetic nodes.

/// The traffic figure a deployable's Live stop reads. Gated by the SITE: a deployable the caller
/// cannot read contributes no rows, which is the same answer a deployable with no traffic gives --
/// so the call is never an existence oracle for somebody else's id.
@sdk
@executor("integration.siteTraffic.inWindow")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Read deployables' traffic over a window, from the aggregate the edge's request log is folded into (epic memql#4906). Answers v1:observability:siteTraffic rows: one per bucket per deployable, or -- with summary -- one per deployable for the whole window, which is what a list needs to say `last served` for twenty of them without pulling twenty series. A DEPLOYABLE WITH NO ROWS IN THE WINDOW GETS NO ROW: an absent figure and a zero are different answers, and the client says `unmeasured` in words. A deployable the caller may not read is dropped, not refused, so the call cannot be used to learn whether an id exists. The cluster's own system-owned surfaces are never recorded, so they are always unmeasured. Bounded at 200 deployables per call, refused past it rather than truncated -- a silently short answer reads as `those have no traffic`.")
builtin siteTrafficInWindow {
  siteIds      []string!  @description("The deployables to read, as bare ids.")
  bucket       string!    @description("Which aggregate to read: `1m` for an hour-long window, `1h` for a day or a week.")
  windowStart  string!    @description("Inclusive start, RFC3339. The caller aligns it to the bucket.")
  windowEnd    string!    @description("Exclusive end, RFC3339. Half-open, so two adjacent windows add up.")
  summary      boolean    @description("Fold the whole window into one row per deployable instead of one row per bucket. Summed in the database, which is the point of the mode.")
}

// ---- the deploy pipeline (epic memql#4794) ----
//
// Three capabilities and one shape: each runs under the CALLER's actor,
// resolves its package through the owner-scoped packageById first, and is
// therefore refused by name for a caller who does not own it. The pipeline's
// own writes are @serverOnly mutations the Go side stamps internal origin on;
// none of that is expressible here, and none of it needs to be.

@sdk
@executor("integration.packages.analyze")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Analyze a package source offline and return the report, deploying nothing (epic memql#4794, D12). Fetches the tracked source, walks the manifest, discovers the DSL domains and runs the SAME Init-grade gates strict boot runs -- so 'this DSL would refuse boot' is an answer produced here, before a pod is ever asked to run it. Returns {report, ok}: the report names every deployable with its build plan (or 'prebuilt output found -- build skipped'), every DSL domain with construct counts, any Go pack as reported-not-deployable, and every problem found. A refusal carries one of the stable codes in component/packages/refusal.go.")
builtin packageAnalyze {
  packageId  string!  @description("The v1:platform:package row to analyze.")
}

@sdk
@executor("integration.packages.deploy")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Run one deployment attempt for a package (epic memql#4794). WITHOUT confirm the run parks at awaiting_confirm with the analysis report on a new deployment row and nothing else happens -- that gate is always present (D12), and a redeploy passes it in one click. WITH confirm the run builds, stages, rolls and publishes in the D6 order: a failure anywhere before publish leaves every site serving exactly what it was serving, and a package with no DSL (or unchanged DSL) skips stage and roll entirely so nothing restarts. A package carrying DSL requires the cluster-owner actor and is refused with dsl_requires_cluster_owner at the START, before any build. placements is read only for a deployable's FIRST deploy (epic memql#4885, D8); later deploys find the site through (packageId, packageDeployableName) and never re-ask. Returns {deploymentId, status, awaitingConfirm, deployables, report}; each deployables entry carries {name, siteId, hostname, bundleRef, version, created} on success, {name, refusal} for a half that was refused or skipped, plus accountId / ownDomain for the placement halves that landed and accountRefusal / domainRefusal for the ones the guards refused.")
builtin packageDeploy {
  packageId   string!  @description("The v1:platform:package row to deploy.")
  confirm     boolean  @description("Pass true to proceed past the confirm gate. Absent or false parks the run with its report and returns.")
  placements  object   @description("Where each deployable goes on its FIRST deploy: an object keyed by deployable name, each value {hostname, accountId, ownDomain}. hostname is the site's own hostname under the cluster domain and is required for a never-deployed app (deployable_binding_missing otherwise); accountId ties the site to the client it is for; ownDomain binds the client's own domain. The pipeline applies the two optional halves itself after the site exists, as the same updateSiteAccount and customDomainAdd calls the page makes, under the caller's actor -- so the existing guards decide, and a refused one lands on the outcome without failing the publish. Chosen once and remembered on the site row.")
}

@sdk
@executor("integration.packages.rollback")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Restore a package to what an earlier successful deployment left live (epic memql#4794, D7). Executes the D6 order REVERSED -- every site is pointed back at its recorded bundle version FIRST, then the active-set pointer goes back and the cluster rolls -- so the app stops using the old schema before the schema changes, which is the window the forward order closes in the other direction. Restores a tuple recorded on the prior row rather than replaying events, which is why the timeline is append-only. Rolling back to a deployment that carried DSL requires the cluster-owner actor. Returns {restored}.")
builtin packageRollback {
  packageId     string!  @description("The v1:platform:package to roll back.")
  deploymentId  string!  @description("The earlier v1:platform:packageDeployment whose state to restore. Must have finished as succeeded.")
}

// ---- update detection, the D11 feeds (epic memql#4794) ----
//
// TWO FEEDS, ONE EFFECT: set latestKnownVersion + updateAvailable on the
// package row, which broadcasts and lights the OS cue. NEITHER FEED EVER
// DEPLOYS. Deploying the update is a person's click starting a new deployment
// run at the new version, through analyze and confirm again -- so a feed that
// could write anything else would be deciding to deploy on somebody's behalf.
//
// The webhook feed rides the EXISTING inbound seam (memql#2957: deny-by-default
// source allowlist plus per-source HMAC) rather than a new HTTP route, and it
// reads a row that seam already staged -- which is the "product DSL reacts with
// an ordinary automation on node.created" shape v1:platform:inboundRequest's
// own doc describes.

@executor("integration.packages.noteUpstreamFromWebhook")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Match a staged inbound webhook delivery to the packages tracking that repository and record the version it announced (epic memql#4794, D11). Reads a v1:platform:inboundRequest row the inbound receiver already verified; a delivery whose repository matches no package is a NO-OP rather than an error, because most of a cluster's webhooks are not about packages. Writes only latestKnownVersion and updateAvailable, and never starts a deployment.")
builtin packageNoteUpstreamFromWebhook {
  inboundRequestId  string!  @description("The v1:platform:inboundRequest row staged by the inbound receiver.")
  source            string   @description("The allowlisted source segment the delivery arrived on.")
  body              string   @description("The verified raw body, exactly as signed.")
}

@executor("integration.packages.pollUpstream")
@args(profile="object")
@description("The D11 polling fallback: walk every repo-sourced package and compare the upstream head against what is deployed (epic memql#4794). Runs under each package's own credential where one is named (credentialId), unsealed at call time under the PACKAGE OWNER's actor and stored nowhere; a package whose credential cannot be resolved -- revoked, or not its owner's -- is skipped with a warning rather than polled anonymously, so a private repository never reads as unchanged because the request answered 404. Writes only latestKnownVersion and updateAvailable, and only for a package whose upstream actually moved -- a package that has not changed is not written, so the OS cue does not flicker and the mesh does not carry an event per package per poll.")
builtin packagePollUpstream {
}

// ---- personal source credentials (epic memql#4885, D10) ----
//
// The write half of v1:platform:sourceCredential, and the reason the create is
// a capability rather than the browser calling a mutation: a secret cannot be
// sealed in a browser. secret.Encrypt runs under MEMQL_MASTER_KEY, a key that
// exists on nodes and must never exist on a laptop, so what crosses the wire is
// the plaintext, once, over the same TLS-terminated stream every other call
// uses -- and it is never sent back. The same argument integrations/email's
// configure capability records for a vendor key.
//
// AUTHORIZATION IS NOT IN THESE DECLARATIONS. Both run under the CALLER's
// actor: the create stamps the owner from it and refuses an empty one, and the
// revoke is an owned mutation the write guard admits for the row's owner.

@sdk
@executor("integration.packages.sourceCredentialCreate")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Store a personal source credential (epic memql#4885, D10). The token crosses the wire ONCE, inside this call, is sealed server-side under MEMQL_MASTER_KEY, and lands on a v1:platform:sourceCredential row owned by the caller; it appears in no row, no log line and no reply. Only github.com is admitted as a host today -- any other host is refused with source_host_unsupported, and the answer is to upload the tree as a zip instead. Returns {credentialId, fingerprint}, the fingerprint being the token's last four characters prefixed with '...', for telling two credentials apart. Name the credential on a package through createPackage or updatePackageSource; the fetcher resolves it under that package's OWNER, so a package naming somebody else's credential is refused by name.")
builtin sourceCredentialCreate {
  host   string!  @description("The host the token authenticates against. github.com is the only value admitted today.")
  label  string!  @description("The person's own name for it, e.g. 'work laptop'. Display only.")
  token  string!  @description("The access token itself. Read once, sealed, and discarded; never stored or echoed in the clear.")
}

@sdk
@executor("integration.packages.sourceCredentialRevoke")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Revoke one of the caller's source credentials (epic memql#4885, D10). Flips status to revoked and stamps revokedAt through the owned revokeSourceCredential mutation, so the write guard admits the row's owner (or a cluster owner) and nobody else. The row is never deleted -- it is the audit history of what fetched under it -- and every source fetching under it refuses at its next fetch with credential_revoked until it is switched to another credential on its Source stop. Returns {credentialId, status}.")
builtin sourceCredentialRevoke {
  credentialId  string!  @description("The v1:platform:sourceCredential row to revoke.")
}

// ---- the compose probes (epic memql#4885, D11) ----
//
// Two questions the Source stop asks before a person commits to a source, and
// the answer to each is a TYPED REASON rather than a row: neither probe writes
// anything, and neither stamps anything -- not even the credential heartbeat a
// fetch records, because a probe is a question and a question is not a use.
//
// AUTHORIZATION IS NOT IN THESE DECLARATIONS. Both run under the CALLER's
// actor: the source probe resolves the named credential exactly as a fetch
// would, so a credential the caller cannot read is answered as a reason before
// any request leaves the cluster; the artifact probe reads the zip through the
// same owner-scoped Library read the deploy uses.

@sdk
@executor("integration.packages.sourceProbe")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Ask whether this cluster can read a repository (epic memql#4885, D11). Parses the URL, resolves credentialId under the caller's own actor the way a fetch does, asks GitHub for the repository, and answers {host, reachable, private, defaultBranch, reason}. reason is exactly one of: ok (reachable; private and defaultBranch are GitHub's answer), not_found_or_private (404 with no credential -- GitHub answers the two alike, and the stop offers a credential), credential_cannot_see_it (refused under the credential: choose another, or fix its grant), credential_not_found (the caller cannot read the named credential), credential_revoked, source_host_unsupported (only github.com today, or upload a zip), rate_limited (ask again later). A typed reason, never the API's own body. A GitHub this cluster cannot reach is an ERROR rather than a reason, so the stop says so and stays editable; the fetch is the authority and the probe is a courtesy. Writes nothing and stamps nothing.")
builtin sourceProbe {
  repoUrl       string!  @description("The repository URL as typed, e.g. https://github.com/acme/widget.")
  credentialId  string   @description("One of the caller's v1:platform:sourceCredential rows to probe under. Empty probes anonymously, which is what a public repository needs.")
}

@sdk
@executor("integration.packages.artifactProbe")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Ask what kind of tree a zip in the caller's Library is (epic memql#4885, D11). Opens it through the same fetch a deploy uses -- the caller's own bytes, expanded by OpenZip under the packages limits, so a zip the deploy would refuse is refused here by the same code -- and answers {isPackage, isBuiltSite, fileCount, totalBytes}. isPackage: memql-package.yaml sits at the root, and the package path takes it from here. isBuiltSite: index.html sits at the root and there is no manifest, and the hand-made path takes it and asks for the kind. Neither: the zip is something else, commonly a folder wrapping the site one level down. fileCount and totalBytes are counted over the opened tree. Writes nothing; the artifact stays exactly the Library row it was.")
builtin artifactProbe {
  artifactId  string!  @description("The v1:library:artifact index row of the zip to probe.")
}
